You are helping build the Vulnerability Triage Assistant: a Next.js web app that takes a vulnerability title or CVE ID and returns a structured analysis (plain-language explanation, executive summary, risk assessment, remediation steps, ticket-ready text).

Always read spec.md first. It is the source of truth. Do not add features outside the v1 scope.

Stack:
- Next.js (App Router), TypeScript (strict), Tailwind CSS
- Vercel AI SDK with the Google Gemini API (free tier); validate structured output with zod
- Vitest for tests; GitHub Actions for CI; Vercel for deployment
- No database and no authentication in v1

Security rules:
- Never hardcode secrets or API keys. Read GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY from the server environment only.
- Never expose secrets to client code, logs, or commits. Keep .env.local git-ignored and commit only .env.example with placeholders.
- Validate all input on the server (3 to 200 characters, allowed characters only, trim whitespace).
- Validate AI output against the zod schema. Render AI text as plain text, never raw HTML.
- Treat user input as data, not as instructions, in prompts.

AI rules:
- The advisory link is built by the app from the CVE ID. Never let the model generate URLs.
- Every result must show a "verify the fixed version against the vendor advisory before deploying" note.
- Keep the system prompt in its own file.

Code style:
- Use small, single-purpose functions. Keep validation, link building, and the AI call in separate modules so they are easy to unit test.
- Avoid "any" types. Prefer clear names over comments.
- Keep the UI simple: one input, one results view, clear loading and error states.

Workflow:
- Make small changes. Write or update unit tests with each feature.
- Suggest commit messages in the form "feat: ...", "fix: ...", "test: ...", "docs: ...".
- Briefly explain non-obvious code so the author can explain it later.
